# CITATION.cff
#
# This file lets GitHub show a "Cite this repository" button on the repo
# page, and lets Zenodo (see ZENODO_SETUP.md) pull correct metadata when
# archiving a release.
#
# IMPORTANT — placeholders to check before committing:
#   - `given-names` / `family-names`: confirm exact spelling / preferred form
#   - `orcid`: add if you have one (https://orcid.org) — strongly
#     recommended for a citable scientific/technical tool; delete the line
#     if you don't have one yet
#   - `version` / `date-released`: keep in sync with the actual latest
#     GitHub release every time you cut one (this file does not update
#     itself)
#   - `doi`: leave the line commented out until Zenodo has minted one (see
#     ZENODO_SETUP.md), then uncomment and fill in

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "PyCuDAL: A Python Implementation of Parametric Acceptance Limits for USP <905> Content Uniformity and USP <711> Dissolution"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Moaz
    family-names: Elessawey
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/moazelessawey/pycudal"
url: "https://github.com/moazelessawey/pycudal"
license: MIT
version: "1.0.8"
date-released: "2026-08-26"
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
keywords:
  - pharmaceutical validation
  - USP 905
  - USP 711
  - content uniformity
  - dissolution
  - parametric tolerance interval
  - acceptance limits
  - computer system validation
abstract: >
  PyCuDAL is an open-source Python re-implementation of CuDAL (Content
  Uniformity and Dissolution Acceptance Limits), a legacy SAS/AF system
  used to calculate parametric (tolerance-interval) acceptance limits and
  pass-probability estimates for USP <905> Uniformity of Dosage Units
  (Content Uniformity) and USP <711> Dissolution. Every formula has been
  verified against the original SAS macros and cross-checked with an
  independent R implementation. The package includes a command-line
  interface and two graphical interfaces (Tkinter and optional PySide6).
